	"identifying_description": "Linen lined with cotton. Edges bound with narrow white silk bias. Crewel embroidery, quilting done in silk thread. Ground fabric worked in backstitched quilting in a design with boteh and flowers. The center panel is embroidered in wool and silk crewel work with he images show Apollo's empty chariot in the clouds, with two doves attached to reigns and looking back at him. He, in his Roman centurion uniform, dropping his helmet, has fallen from the chariot and is grasping the hand of a languid nymph (Daphne?) who doesn't look too enthusiastic. Eros hovers overhead, aiming his bow at them. Below are Eros' cupids, forging more arrows of love at a forge and anvil.   Corners appear to show a monogram (ADC ?)",
	"web_text": "- From the Exotic to the Mystical: May 4, 2013 - August 4, 2013: Foreign and mythological elements intertwine in this bed cover. The finely quilted ground is worked in a boteh pattern, a motif of Indo-Persian origin that became popular in the West during the eighteenth century with the influx of Kashmir shawls. The central panel depicts a story from Greek mythology. Apollo, in his centurion uniform, begs the unmoved Daphne as Eros hovers above, having just shot arrows inciting love in him and hatred in her. Cupids and Cyclops forge more arrows in Hephaestus's workshop below.",
	"didactic_label": "Foreign and mythological elements intertwine in this bed cover. The finely quilted ground is worked in a boteh pattern, a motif of Indo-Persian origin that became popular in the West during the eighteenth century with the influx of Kashmir shawls. The central panel depicts a story from Greek mythology. Apollo, in his centurion uniform, begs the unmoved Daphne as\nEros hovers above, having just shot arrows inciting love in him and hatred in her. Cupids and Cyclops forge more arrows in Hephaestus's workshop below.",
	"exhibition_history_text": "From the Exotic to the Mystical: Textile Treasures from the Permanent Collection, deY, 5/4 - 8/4/2013",
